{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1788296644,"nonce":"L29eisqAPrfpO3hDK1M4ql4v7Ok=","title":"work-cover {dry_run:true} reports coverage_after as the coverage BEFORE the repair, so a dry run's verdict is always 'still broken'","message":"Found on first bench contact with `work-cover`, session 21, bench `_RimWorld-Agent` `20260901T205859`, assembly `1.0.0+52606d1`. Raw envelopes at `accept/runs/s21-20260901/12-workcover-dryrun.json` and `13-workcover-real.json`.\n\n## What happened\n\n`work-cover {dry_run:true}` and `work-cover` were run back to back against the same colony (three colonists, Doctor `have:1` against `floor:2`). Both correctly picked Harrell — highest `AverageOfRelevantSkillsFor(Doctor)`, cited to `RimWorld/Pawn_WorkSettings.EnableAndInitialize`'s own order. They disagree on the field that decides whether an agent commits.\n\n| | `repaired` | `coverage_after.ok` | `coverage_after.under` | Doctor row |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| `dry_run:true` | Harrell, before 0 after 3 | **`false`** | **`[\"Doctor\"]`** | `have:1, short_by:1, …` |\n| real | Harrell, before 0 after 3 | `true` | `[]` | `have:2` |\n\nThe real run is right, and the digest independently agrees (`digest.work_coverage.ok` flipped to `true`, `under:[]`).\n\n**In a dry run, `coverage_after` reports the coverage BEFORE the repair, under a name that promises the coverage after it.**\n\n## Why this is a defect and not a naming quibble\n\nA dry run exists to answer exactly one question — *would this fix it?* — and this field answers *is it fixed?*, which in a dry run is always no by construction. An agent that runs `work-cover {dry_run:true}`, reads `coverage_after.ok: false` and `under: [\"Doctor\"]`, and concludes the repair is insufficient, has been told the opposite of the truth by the only field that addresses its question. The `repaired` list is right there and contradicts it, so the envelope is internally inconsistent as well as wrong.\n\nThis matters more than an ordinary read defect because of what `work-cover` is for. `40ed42f` exists because the M1 run's only doctor was its first casualty; the repair is meant to be reached for under time pressure, and `dry_run` is the safe way to reach for it. A dry run whose verdict field says \"still broken\" trains the agent not to trust the verb.\n\n## Scope\n\n`coverage_after` in a dry run must be the PROJECTED coverage — recompute over the roster as it would stand with the promotions in `repaired` applied — or the field must be named for what it actually is and a projected one added beside it. Prefer the projection: it is the question the caller asked, `WorkCoverage` already has the computation, and the promotion list is already in hand.\n\nCheck `still_under` for the same bug: it was `[]` in both runs here, which is right for the real run and, if it is the pre-repair value, accidentally right for the dry run only because the single promotion happened to clear the only shortfall. A colony two doctors short would show whether it projects or reports.\n\nRead `Source/AutoRimmer/PawnManageVerbs.cs` `work-cover` and `Source/AutoRimmer/WorkCoverage.cs`; the same builder serves `digest.work_coverage`, so whatever is done must not change the digest's meaning, where \"coverage now\" is the only correct reading.\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- `work-cover {dry_run:true}` on an under-covered colony reports a `coverage_after` that reflects the promotions in `repaired`, and it matches what the subsequent real run reports.\n- Proven on a colony short by TWO, so a partial repair is distinguishable from a complete one: `still_under` must be non-empty in the dry run when the promotions available cannot reach the floor.\n- The dry run mutates nothing: `digest.work_coverage` is byte-identical before and after it.\n- The real run's `coverage_after` continues to agree with a following `digest` read.","files":null}]}